WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.962
Do you ever wonder what would happen
if we just disappeared

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:07.298
off the face of the earth?

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:12.429
I mean, as a species, if one day we just

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:17.058
didn't exist anymore,

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:25.900
what do you think would happen?

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.613
A portion of this video
is sponsored by HP.

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:33.783
So I used to live in Toronto

00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:37.245
and there was a building,
but I used to spend a lot of time in.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.955
I used to study there.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:41.708
I did my research there
and I did my thesis there.

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:46.421
But a lot of people hate it,
this building.

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:47.797
Like they hate it.

00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:51.134
This building with a passion.

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:57.098
It was one of those buildings
that everyone had an opinion

00:00:57.098 --> 00:01:01.311
about because when you walk by it,
it's impossible to ignore it.

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:07.400
It's this massive block of concrete
and it has a very dominating presence.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:13.114
It has this attitude of I don't
give a crap what anyone thinks about me,

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:17.619
which is kind of a bizarre idea.

00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:20.705
Buildings are designed to impress people.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:24.459
And, you know, as architects,
we go through so many public

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:28.338
consultations, you know,
sometimes it's a little bit of a overkill.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.217
But, you know, people actually have a say

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.260
in what the building
is supposed to look like.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.264
And they have a say all the way down
to the color of the building.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:42.560
I remember when I was working
at my very first job here in Vancouver,

00:01:42.852 --> 00:01:46.481
we had this white
brick building in Chinatown,

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:50.235
and the Chinatown council said, No,
we don't want this.

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:53.905
White represents death in Chinese culture.

00:01:53.905 --> 00:02:01.663
And so they wanted us to change the color
of the building.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:05.208
So public opinion,
you know, whether you like it or not,

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:09.170
it does have an impact on the building's
design.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.424
The building has to kind of justify
their presence in some way.

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:16.469
They actually did talk
about removing this building a lot,

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:21.891
but apparently it would take the force
of an atomic bomb to destroy this thing

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:26.437
and it would cost more
and it would do more environmental damage

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:28.731
to demolish it than to just renovated.

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:34.404
And so they ended up just slapping
on a glass facade and cover it up.

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:36.489
From a energy consumption point of view,

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:39.701
this building is just an energy
consuming monster.

00:02:40.243 --> 00:02:43.329
It's poorly insulated,
is poorly waterproofed.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.625
This thing is just one big thermal bridge
for those of you

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:51.713
who don't know how cold it gets in
Toronto, it gets have you ever again cold?

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:54.716
It comes down to -30 degrees.

00:02:54.883 --> 00:02:55.842
It's brutal.

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.052
It's also one of the reasons
why I left Toronto.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:02.182
Also, from a carbon footprint
point of view, concrete consumes

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.976
so much carbon when it's being built.

00:03:05.768 --> 00:03:08.438
This building is just so wrong in
so many ways.

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:13.109
But there is a small group of people
who seem to really love this building

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:18.239
and just brutalist architecture in general
and I do think I get the appeal.

00:03:18.948 --> 00:03:22.869
It's the lack of pretentiousness
and it's the integrity

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:27.165
and it's the I don't give a crap attitude
because in the end,

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.335
this building is going to be here
long after

00:03:30.335 --> 00:03:33.254
my tiny little existence,

00:03:33.796 --> 00:03:36.799
and it's going to be here
long after we're gone.

00:03:36.925 --> 00:04:33.481
We are actually the temporary visitors
to this building.

00:04:33.481 --> 00:04:37.026
The thing that surprised me
the most is just the sheer

00:04:37.026 --> 00:04:39.904
speed in which nature starts to take over.

00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:42.949
Apparently,
just in the first couple of years,

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:53.501
native animal species start to come back.

00:04:56.045 --> 00:04:56.504
Did you know

00:04:56.504 --> 00:05:00.258
that the Blue heron
is a native species to Toronto?

00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:03.136
I don't think I've ever seen
a blue heron in Toronto for

